{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2529","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2529","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2529\",\"parent_revision\":\"4656722964dbac523bb7ee2e0336fe57819beb084f36f4861b4d929530dc6339\",\"root_revision\":\"4656722964dbac523bb7ee2e0336fe57819beb084f36f4861b4d929530dc6339\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThe current `backlog_view` progressive disclosure contract (map/navigate/extract) operates only at two levels: GitHub issue section (Level 1) and entry within section (Level 2). It does not descend into the markdown content of an entry to expose sub-headings as individually navigable nodes.\\n\\nEvery structured report stored in the system \u2014 RT-ICA assessments, groomed sections, acceptance criteria, design decisions \u2014 uses `###` sub-headings as the natural granularity for navigation. An agent requesting a section currently receives the entire markdown blob, facing the same context-overflow problem the disclosure contract was designed to solve.\\n\\nAdditionally, code fences within sections are returned as inline text rather than as individually referenceable units. An agent cannot retrieve a specific code block without receiving all surrounding context.\\n\\n**Required behavior:**\\n\\n1. **Recursive sub-section navigation**: The map must show sub-sections as ordinals when a section contains `##` or `###` sub-headings. When a sub-section is navigated and itself contains further sub-headings, the behavior must be identical to navigating a top-level section \u2014 the agent receives a map of its children and can navigate/extract any of them. There is no fixed depth limit; recursion follows the actual document structure.\\n\\n2. **Code-fence extraction**: Within any section or sub-section returned via `navigate`, code fences must be extracted from the inline text and replaced with a navigation token (e.g. `[code:4.0.code.0]`). The raw code block content must be individually accessible via its own ordinal. This allows agents to read the structural context of a section without receiving large code blocks inline, then fetch specific code blocks by reference when needed.\\n\\n3. **Backend-agnostic placement**: This extraction and navigation layer sits between the backend (GitHub, SQLite, memory, beads) and the MCP tool interface. The backends return raw content; the disclosure layer applies the recursive structure and code-fence extraction uniformly regardless of which backend is active.\\n\\n**Observed impact**: Without this, #2515 (sam_plan read/ready) is still blocked \u2014 plan sections contain sub-structured reports that cannot be navigated below the section level. The entire progressive disclosure feature fails its primary use case for the content shapes that actually exist in the system.\\n\\n**User-provided context**: \\\"Given that there are multiple backend types. And this sits between the backend and the MCP tool interface. It needs to handle all levels of section headings. When requesting a sub section, if that subsection has subsections... then the behaviour should be the same as if it was the top section. Navigation is supplied, code fences are extracted and replaced with a navigation token to view them individually.\\\"\\n\\n## Fact-Check\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nCLAIM: \\\"The current `backlog_view` progressive disclosure contract operates only at two levels: GitHub issue section (Level 1) and entry within section (Level 2).\\\"\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\nEVIDENCE:\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/ordinal_mapper.py, lines 141-144, 196-269\\n  - Retrieved: 2026-06-01\\n  - Content: OrdinalPathMapper.build_map() produces level-1 entries (one per section) and level-2 entries (one per entry within section when emission gate fires). No level-3 or deeper ordinals generated. Docstring: \\\"Ordinal levels: Level 1 (\\\"N\\\"): section index in the normalized list (0-based). Level 2 (\\\"N.M\\\"): entry index M within section N.\\\"\\n\\n---\\n\\nCLAIM: \\\"Code fences within sections are returned as inline text rather than as individually referenceable units.\\\"\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\nEVIDENCE:\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/ordinal_mapper.py, lines 250-268\\n  - Retrieved: 2026-06-01\\n  - Content: Entry content is assigned as `entry_content = entry.content` \u2014 raw markdown blob. NormalizedEntry.content is never parsed for code fences. Code fence parsing exists in progressive_markdown/indexer.py but is not applied to entry bodies in the ordinal resolution path.\\n\\n---\\n\\nCLAIM: \\\"Without this, #2515 is blocked \u2014 plan sections contain sub-structured reports that cannot be navigated below the section level.\\\"\\nVERDICT: REFUTED\\nEVIDENCE:\\n  - Source: backlog_view(selector=\\\"#2515\\\"), Description section\\n  - Retrieved: 2026-06-01\\n  - Content: Item #2515 explicitly states \\\"Depends on: #2521 (MCP progressive disclosure contract \u2014 platform standard this item implements first)\\\" \u2014 NOT #2529. Requirements state \\\"add a compact flag (returns plan metadata + task IDs without full task bodies) or offset/limit for task-level pagination, consistent with the `backlog_view` pattern.\\\" No mention of requiring Level 3+ navigation.\\nFALSIFICATION_RESULT: The claim asserts #2529 is a blocker for #2515. Backlog item #2515 declares dependency on #2521, not #2529. Item description does not mention needing recursive sub-section navigation.\\n\\n---\\n\\nCLAIM: \\\"progressive_markdown/indexer.py has a MarkdownIndexer with heading stack algorithm that is reusable for entry sub-parsing.\\\"\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\nEVIDENCE:\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/progressive_markdown/indexer.py, lines 135-147, 186-212\\n  - Retrieved: 2026-06-01\\n  - Content: Class MarkdownIndexer (line 135) implements heading stack algorithm. Docstring (lines 139-142): \\\"Uses a heading stack algorithm compatible with documents that skip heading levels or have irregular nesting.\\\" Method _process_heading_token (lines 186-212) manages open_stack of section builders per heading level. Algorithm is extractable for entry content sub-parsing.\\n\\n---\\n\\nCLAIM: \\\"The ordinal validation regex `^(\\\\d+\\\\.)*\\\\d+$` accepts arbitrary depth.\\\"\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\nEVIDENCE:\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/disclosure_handler.py, line 33\\n  - Retrieved: 2026-06-01\\n  - Content: _ORDINAL_PATTERN = re.compile(r\\\"^(\\\\d+\\\\.)*\\\\d+$\\\"). Pattern breakdown: ^ (start), (\\\\d+\\\\.)* (zero or more digit-dot sequences = arbitrary depth), \\\\d+ (trailing digits), $ (end). Accepts \\\"0\\\", \\\"4.0\\\", \\\"3.0.0\\\", \\\"1.2.3.4.5\\\" without limit on depth.\\n</div>\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Final: feat: Recursive sub-section and code-fence navigation for backlog_view progressive disclosure\\nDate: 2026-06-01\\nGoal: Extend backlog_view progressive disclosure to support recursive sub-heading navigation and code-fence extraction within section content, with no fixed depth limit\\n\\nConditions:\\n1. Current ordinal structure handles exactly 2 levels (section N, entry N.M) | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Verified in ordinal_mapper.py build_map() \u2014 terminates at entry level\\n2. NormalizedEntry.content stores raw markdown that may contain ## or ### sub-headings | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Confirmed from content_normalizer.py NormalizedEntry dataclass\\n3. Ordinal extension format for recursive sub-sections (e.g. N.M.K, N.M.K.L) | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Ordinal regex ^(\\\\d+\\\\.)*\\\\d+$ already accepts arbitrary depth \u2014 verified in disclosure_handler.py\\n4. Code-fence token format (mixed alphanumeric: 4.0.code.0) | Snapshot: MISSING \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: User decision 2026-06-01 \u2014 mixed alphanumeric format 4.0.code.0; parser must be extended to accept \\\"code\\\" as a named segment\\n5. Extraction layer position: within disclosure_handler.py between backend and MCP interface | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Confirmed from disclosure_handler.py and alignment-analyst \u2014 backend-agnostic pattern already established\\n6. No fixed depth limit \u2014 recursion follows actual document structure | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Confirmed from item description and alignment-analyst \u2014 intentional by design\\n7. Existing disclosure test coverage patterns and test file locations | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Wave 0 \u2014 groomed_body_doc fixture already has ### sub-headings; test files at backlog_core/tests/unit/test_ordinal_mapper.py, test_disclosure_handler.py\\n8. progressive_markdown module markdown parsing capabilities | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Fact-check VERIFIED \u2014 MarkdownIndexer with heading stack algorithm confirmed in progressive_markdown/indexer.py\\n9. Backward compatibility: existing N.M ordinals must still resolve correctly | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Auto-resolved \u2014 extension semantics require additive ordinal scheme; AC added\\n10. Impact on MCP tool docs, agent instructions, and consumers | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Impact Radius written \u2014 docs/mcp-progressive-disclosure-contract.md and backlog-mcp-validator.md identified as update targets\\nA. CI testpath gap \u2014 backlog_core/tests/ excluded from CI testpaths | Final: AVAILABLE | Source: User decision 2026-06-01 \u2014 fix included in this item's scope\\nB. Code-fence ordinal regex incompatibility | Final: AVAILABLE | Source: User decision 2026-06-01 \u2014 mixed alphanumeric accepted; parser extension required\\n\\nChanges from snapshot:\\n- Condition 4: MISSING \u2192 AVAILABLE (user decision: mixed alphanumeric 4.0.code.0)\\n- Conditions 7-10: DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE (resolved by Wave 0/1 agents)\\n- Condition A (new): AVAILABLE (user confirmed fix in scope)\\n- Condition B (new): AVAILABLE (user confirmed mixed alphanumeric format)\\n- Claim \\\"#2515 blocked by this\\\" \u2192 REFUTED by fact-checker (#2515 depends on #2521)\\n\\nAVAILABLE count: 12\\nDERIVABLE count: 0\\nMISSING count: 0\\n\\nDecision: APPROVED\\n</div>\\n\\n## Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n**Alignment assessment**: ALIGNED\\n\\n### Mission Statement Cited\\n\\nFrom `plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md`:\\n\\n> \\\"Language-agnostic development process harness that orchestrates feature development through a structured 7-stage pipeline. Every stage produces an MCP-registered artifact (stateless handoff) \u2014 stored via `artifact_register` and retrieved via `artifact_read`, never via direct filesystem paths.\\\"\\n\\nAnd from design principles:\\n\\n> \\\"Every stage produces an MCP-registered artifact (stateless handoff) \u2014 stored via `artifact_register` and retrieved via `artifact_read`, never via direct filesystem paths\\\"\\n\\n### Historical Direction\\n\\n**Merged PRs establishing progressive disclosure architecture:**\\n\\n| Commit | Title | Pattern Established |\\n|--------|-------|-------------------|\\n| d23cceb5 | `feat(backlog_core): add OrdinalPathMapper for dot-path ordinal mapping` | 2-level ordinal structure (section N, entry N.M) with extensible regex `^(\\\\d+\\\\.)*\\\\d+$` accepting arbitrary depth. |\\n| 0606321c | `feat(backlog_core): add map/navigate/head/skip_tokens to backlog_view tool` | Progressive disclosure contract with Phase 4 three-layer navigation (map/navigate/extract). Cites architect spec \u00a74.6, \u00a75.7, \u00a79.2. |\\n| 7f29e2c0 | `feat(backlog_core): add BacklogViewDisclosureHandler with un-gated content path` | Extraction layer pattern between backend and MCP interface; backend-agnostic. |\\n| d23cceb5 | `feat(backlog_core): add OrdinalPathMapper...` | Level-2 emission gate (architect spec \u00a75.6): `entry_count > 1 OR section_est_tokens > TOKEN_BUDGET` \u2014 cites token budget constraints as the limiting factor, not architecture. |\\n\\n**Direction signal**: All recent work has incrementally built progressive disclosure with emphasis on removing artificial context limits. The ordinal regex was designed to accept unlimited depth. The emit gate is based on token budget, not structural depth.\\n\\n### Analysis\\n\\n**Feature extends established architecture consistently:**\\n\\n- **Recursive sub-heading navigation (any depth)**: The ordinal_mapper.py implementation (commit d23cceb5) introduced the regex `^(\\\\d+\\\\.)*\\\\d+$` which accepts dot-paths of arbitrary depth (e.g., 1.2.3.4.5). The feature leverages this existing design point by enabling the disclosure handler to generate Level 3+ ordinals (N.M.K, N.M.K.L) for nested headings. This is not a departure\u2014it is the fulfillment of infrastructure already in place.\\n\\n- **Code-fence extraction as addressable ordinals**: Treats code blocks as first-class navigation elements within the ordinal system, consistent with the principle that agents should \\\"always see a menu before committing to reading a chunk\\\" (item description). Code blocks currently force-embed in surrounding prose; extracting them as ordinals applies the same progressive disclosure model consistently.\\n\\n- **Backend-agnostic extraction layer**: Feature explicitly positions the extraction layer between backend and MCP interface. This mirrors the pattern established in commit 7f29e2c0 (BacklogViewDisclosureHandler). The layer consumes normalized section structures and produces ordinals and resolved content\u2014implementation is internal to the tool contract, transparent to backend.\\n\\n**Alignment with \\\"No Invented Limits\\\" principle**:\\n\\nThe current 2-level limit (section N, entry N.M) is an artifact of the initial design, not a principled boundary. Item description states: *\\\"The current contract delivers the top two levels of this; this item delivers the rest.\\\"* This is the natural maturation of the architecture, removing the artificial depth cap. When an agent reading a large structured report (RT-ICA with nested subsections, groomed acceptance criteria, design decisions) encounters a heading with sub-headings, they are currently forced to receive the entire section as one block. The feature removes this constraint, enabling the agent to drill down level-by-level\u2014consistent with the harness's goal to serve agent context budgets efficiently.\\n\\n**Potential ambiguity** (implementation, not mission):\\n\\nThe RT-ICA section identifies MISSING conditions around:\\n- Code-fence token format design (numeric-only vs alphanumeric variants)\\n- Backward compatibility acceptance criteria (N.M ordinals must remain unchanged)\\n- CI testpath configuration (backlog_core/tests/ currently excluded from CI discovery)\\n\\nThese are design and implementation blockers (item is marked BLOCKED), but they do not represent mission divergence. Once design decisions are made, the implementation will still align with the established progressive disclosure architecture.\\n\\n### Summary\\n\\n0 concerns identified \u2014 proposed change is consistent with product mission and historical direction. Feature extends the established progressive disclosure architecture (ordinal_mapper, disclosure_handler, token budget constraints) to its natural conclusion: remove the artificial 2-level depth limit and add code-fence navigation. Both extensions serve the core mission: enable agents to navigate large structured documents without context bloat. The ordinal regex was designed for arbitrary depth; the extraction layer was designed to be backend-agnostic; the emit gate is token-budget-driven, not depth-driven. This feature completes the vision, not diverges from it.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **feat: recursive sub-section and code-fence navigation for backlog_view progressive disclosure** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Post-implementation review \u2014 architecture gap identified 2026-06-01, session with Jamie\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-06-01\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n\\n<!-- artifact-gist:9192b4cc39c307c5b2c5d4fdf57b9f69 -->\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-06-01)\\n\\n### RT-ICA Assessment\\n\\n- Goal: Extend backlog_view progressive disclosure to support recursive sub-section navigation and code-fence extraction, operating at every depth level of markdown content without a fixed depth limit\\n- Status: APPROVED\\n- AVAILABLE:\\n  - OrdinalPathMapper.build_map() source confirmed \u2014 terminates at Level 2 (N.M) today; extension target is clear\\n  - NormalizedEntry.content confirmed as flat raw markdown blob \u2014 sub-headings present but not parsed\\n  - MarkdownIndexer + heading stack algorithm confirmed in progressive_markdown/indexer.py \u2014 directly reusable via ProgressiveMarkdownNavigator.from_markdown(entry.content)\\n  - groomed_body_doc test fixture confirmed to contain entries with ### sub-headings \u2014 usable for level-3 tests\\n  - Backward compatibility path confirmed \u2014 existing N.M ordinals must resolve identically\\n  - Code-fence format confirmed: 4.0.code.0 (user decision)\\n  - Ordinal regex ^(\\\\d+\\\\.)*\\\\d+$ extension needed to accept \\\"code\\\" segment \u2014 scope confirmed\\n  - CI testpath gap confirmed: pyproject.toml testpaths=[\\\"tests\\\",\\\"tests_sam\\\",\\\"tests_backlog\\\"] excludes backlog_core/tests/; CI testpath fix is in scope\\n  - #2515 blocking claim refuted \u2014 #2515 depends on #2521, not #2529\\n  - Coordinate system bridging needed: MarkdownIndexer produces SectionNode IDs; OrdinalPathMapper needs dot-path integers\\n  - Level-3 emission gate needed to keep map output under 2,000 tokens\\n  - Impact radius of 19 affected systems mapped\\n- DERIVABLE: None\\n- MISSING: None\\n\\n### Artifact Classification\\n\\n- Primary artifact type: Feature (MCP tool enhancement)\\n- Behavioral boundary: backlog_view tool call contract \u2014 which ordinal formats are accepted, what content is returned at each level, and how map output exposes sub-heading structure\\n- Domain-semantic evaluation target: Acceptance criteria must prove that (1) sub-section ordinals beyond N.M are accepted and return correct scoped content, (2) code-fence ordinals use the confirmed format 4.0.code.0, (3) existing N.M ordinals resolve to identical content, and (4) CI runs the tests that verify this behavior\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\nN/A \u2014 feature item, not a regression. Observable gap: requesting a section entry via backlog_view(navigate=\\\"4.0\\\") returns the entire section blob including sub-headings and embedded code fences as undifferentiated text. No level-3 ordinal exists for agents to request sub-sections individually.\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n7/10 \u2014 The progressive disclosure contract was introduced to prevent context overflow when reading large structured items. Large groomed items (RT-ICA assessments, acceptance criteria, design decisions) now commonly fill sections with ### sub-headings that themselves exceed a single agent's context budget. Without level-3 navigation, the disclosure contract solves overflow at the section level but leaves the sub-section level unsolved \u2014 which is where the overflow now concentrates.\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- Agents reading large groomed sections receive the entire entry blob regardless of section size \u2014 the context-overflow problem the disclosure contract was designed to solve recurs at the sub-section level\\n- Structured reports with ### sub-headings (RT-ICA assessments, acceptance criteria, groomed design decisions) are inaccessible at sub-heading granularity via the MCP contract\\n- Code fences embedded within section content are returned inline \u2014 agents that need only the code block must receive all surrounding prose\\n- The backlog-mcp-validator agent does not know about level-3 or code-fence ordinal formats \u2014 it will reject valid calls that use these formats until its instructions are updated\\n- backlog_core/tests/ is excluded from CI \u2014 new level-3 and code-fence tests will not run in CI without a testpath fix\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- Agents can navigate directly to a specific ### sub-heading within a section without receiving the entire section blob\\n- Code fences become individually addressable units \u2014 agents can read code without receiving surrounding prose\\n- RT-ICA assessments, groomed acceptance criteria, and multi-section design decisions become fully navigable at every depth level\\n- The disclosure contract fulfills its original promise: agents never receive more than fits in their context budget at any level of document structure\\n- CI gains test coverage for the progressive disclosure unit tests that currently run only locally\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nWhen backlog_view(map=True) is called, the map includes level-3 ordinal entries for each ### sub-heading within section entries that exceed the token budget or contain multiple sub-headings. Code fences within section entries appear in the map as entries with ordinal format N.M.code.K.\\n\\nWhen backlog_view(navigate=\\\"4.0.0\\\") is called, the response returns only the content of the first sub-section under entry 4.0, bounded to that sub-heading's scope. The response does not include content from sibling sub-sections.\\n\\nWhen backlog_view(navigate=\\\"4.0.code.0\\\") is called, the response returns only the content of the first code fence within entry 4.0.\\n\\nExisting calls with N or N.M ordinals return identical content to their pre-change behavior. No existing agent call breaks.\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. Existing N.M ordinals resolve to identical content as before this change \u2014 backlog_view(navigate=\\\"4.0\\\") on any item that existed before this change returns the same content as it did before, byte-for-byte.\\n2. When a section entry contains ### sub-headings, backlog_view(map=True) lists those sub-headings as level-3 ordinals (e.g., 4.0.0, 4.0.1) in the map output.\\n3. backlog_view(navigate=\\\"4.0.0\\\") returns only the content scoped to the first sub-heading under entry 4.0 \u2014 it does not include content from sub-heading 4.0.1 or beyond.\\n4. Code-fence ordinals use format 4.0.code.0 \u2014 the ordinal parser accepts \\\"code\\\" as a segment type; backlog_view(navigate=\\\"4.0.code.0\\\") returns only the first code fence within entry 4.0.\\n5. An invalid code-fence ordinal (e.g., 4.0.code.99) returns the same OrdinalNotFoundError structure as an invalid numeric ordinal \u2014 no new error shape introduced.\\n6. CI testpath covers backlog_core/tests/ \u2014 pyproject.toml testpaths includes backlog_core/tests and the test-python CI job (uv run -q pytest) executes the new level-3 and code-fence tests.\\n7. Recursive navigation works to arbitrary depth \u2014 a section entry containing both ### and #### headings produces correspondingly deeper ordinals; no fixed depth limit is enforced by the implementation.\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| MCP tool | backlog_view (target of this enhancement) |\\n| File | plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/ordinal_mapper.py |\\n| File | plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/disclosure_handler.py |\\n| File | plugins/development-harness/progressive_markdown/indexer.py (MarkdownIndexer \u2014 reuse) |\\n| File | plugins/development-harness/docs/mcp-progressive-disclosure-contract.md (stale \u2014 update required) |\\n| Agent | backlog-mcp-validator (.claude/agents/backlog-mcp-validator.md) |\\n| Backlog | #2449 \u2014 backlog_view token threshold auto-detection bug (related: same tool) |\\n| Backlog | #2515 \u2014 sam_plan read pagination (same progressive disclosure pattern; does not depend on this item) |\\n| Backlog | #1162 \u2014 SectionType for routing (related: section-level navigation) |\\n\\n### Research\\n\\n\\n\\n### Files\\n\\n| File | Role |\\n|------|------|\\n| plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/ordinal_mapper.py | Primary change \u2014 extend OrdinalPathMapper beyond Level 2; extend ordinal regex to accept \\\"code\\\" segment |\\n| plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/disclosure_handler.py | Primary change \u2014 drive level-3 resolution and code-fence extraction |\\n| plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/disclosure_types.py | New types for code-fence response variant |\\n| plugins/development-harness/progressive_markdown/indexer.py | Reuse \u2014 MarkdownIndexer heading stack algorithm (not a change site) |\\n| plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/unit/test_ordinal_mapper.py | Tests \u2014 zero level-3 coverage today; new coverage required |\\n| plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/unit/test_disclosure_handler.py | Tests \u2014 zero level-3 coverage today; new coverage required |\\n| plugins/development-harness/docs/mcp-progressive-disclosure-contract.md | Docs \u2014 stale; 4.0.1 ordinal reference exists; code-fence undocumented |\\n| .claude/agents/backlog-mcp-validator.md | Agent instructions \u2014 may reject code-fence ordinals until updated |\\n| plugins/development-harness/pyproject.toml | CI fix \u2014 testpaths must include backlog_core/tests |\\n| .github/workflows/code-quality.yml | CI fix \u2014 uv run -q pytest must cover backlog_core/tests via testpaths |\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: None \u2014 RT-ICA confirmed all 12 conditions AVAILABLE; #2515 blocking claim REFUTED (#2515 depends on #2521, not this item)\\n- Blocks: None identified at this time\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nHigh \u2014 Requires extending the ordinal coordinate system to level 3+, bridging MarkdownIndexer SectionNode IDs to dot-path integers, implementing a level-3 emission gate (to keep map output under 2,000 tokens), extending the ordinal regex to accept mixed alphanumeric segments (\\\"code\\\"), implementing code-fence extraction, adding new disclosure_types entries, ensuring full backward compatibility with existing N.M ordinals, adding CI testpath coverage for backlog_core/tests, and updating the disclosure contract doc and backlog-mcp-validator agent instructions. Impact radius of 19 affected systems. Design framing (unbounded-design classification) adds planning overhead before implementation can begin.\\n\\n### API State\\n\\n**OrdinalPathMapper** (`backlog_core/ordinal_mapper.py`, lines 138\u2013354, read 2026-06-01):\\n- `build_map() -> list[OrdinalEntry]` \u2014 produces 2-level dot-path ordinals only: level-1 `\\\"N\\\"` = section index, level-2 `\\\"N.M\\\"` = entry index within section.\\n- Level-2 emission gate (architect spec \u00a75.6): `entry_count > 1 OR section_est_tokens > TOKEN_BUDGET`.\\n- `resolve(ordinal: str) -> ResolvedUnit` \u2014 raises `OrdinalNotFoundError` on miss, carrying `valid_ordinals`.\\n- `OrdinalEntry.ordinal: str` \u2014 docstring shows `\\\"4\\\"`, `\\\"4.0\\\"`, `\\\"4.0.1\\\"` (3-level in example), but `build_map()` never emits level-3 today.\\n\\n**DisclosureRequestParser.parse()** (`backlog_core/disclosure_handler.py`, line 33, read 2026-06-01):\\n- [CROSS-ANGLE] Ordinal regex `_ORDINAL_PATTERN = r\\\"^(\\\\d+\\\\.)*\\\\d+$\\\"` already accepts arbitrary-depth ordinals (`\\\"3.0.0\\\"`, `\\\"4.1.2.0\\\"`). The parser does not need to change.\\n\\n**NormalizedEntry** (`backlog_core/content_normalizer.py`, lines 21\u201330, read 2026-06-01):\\n- [CROSS-ANGLE] `content: str` \u2014 documented as \\\"Raw markdown text of the entry.\\\" Flat blob, no sub-structure. No API change required to carry deeper content \u2014 the field already holds whatever raw markdown the entry contains.\\n\\n**Architectural constraints from existing ADRs and spec comments:**\\n- ADR-2: All components share the `ENCODING` singleton from `progressive_markdown.list_navigator` (cl100k_base). Any new level-3 token counting must use the same singleton.\\n- ADR-5: `BacklogViewDisclosureHandler.handle()` calls `operations.view_item()` via module reference \u2014 not direct import. Must be preserved.\\n- `total_est_tokens` in `MapResponse` sums LEVEL-1 only (architect spec \u00a75.2, #2495 regression guard). Adding level-3 ordinals must not break this invariant.\\n- `OrdinalPathMapper` is stateful per-item; fresh instance per `handle()` call (architect spec \u00a74.4). Recursive sub-heading state must be isolated within a single `build_map()` call.\\n\\n**Changelog:** Internal module, no external changelog. Pipeline phases: T14 (OrdinalPathMapper), T16 (DisclosureRequestParser), T17/T18 (TokenBoundedExtractor + BacklogViewDisclosureHandler). Source: module docstrings, read 2026-06-01.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Agent Instructions\\n\\n- `.claude/agents/backlog-mcp-validator.md` \u2014 validates the MCP backlog_view tool schema including the `map` parameter. Documents valid ordinal format as `^(\\\\d+\\\\.)*\\\\d+$`. Code-fence navigation tokens (if they contain non-digit characters like `\\\"code\\\"`) may not match this pattern \u2014 validator may reject them. Needs update to document code-fence token format and any new response shape. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: MCP validator enforces schema compliance; if new token formats or response fields are emitted, validators will flag correct responses as invalid.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/issue-first.md`,