CODING` and `TOKEN_BUDGET` singletons imported by `ordinal_mapper.py` (ADR-2). New level-3 token counting must reuse the same `ENCODING` singleton. | Risk: LOW | Why: No change to `list_navigator.py` itself; risk is that a new token-count path in `ordinal_mapper.py` bypasses the singleton.\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Other References\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/progressive_markdown/indexer.py::MarkdownIndexer` \u2014 **reuse target**, not a change site. `MarkdownIndexer.build()` + `MarkdownItParser.parse()` provide the recursive heading-tree builder. `ProgressiveMarkdownNavigator.from_markdown(markdown: str)` (navigator.py lines 113\u2013131) is the factory entry point. If called from `ordinal_mapper.py`, this introduces a new import dependency from `backlog_core` into `progressive_markdown`. Cross-bundle import safety requires both packages remain within `plugins/development-harness/`. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: New transitive dependency added to `ordinal_mapper.py`; must remain within the plugin bundle; introduces markdown-it-py parsing into the OrdinalPathMapper call path which adds latency and a new failure mode (parse errors).\\n- `plugins/development-harness/progressive_markdown/__init__.py` \u2014 exports `MarkdownIndexer`, `SectionNode`, `MarkdownItParser`; already used by `indexer_decomposition` tests. No change expected, but verify `__all__` exposes any new symbols needed by `ordinal_mapper.py`. | Risk: LOW | Why: Additive usage only.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/progressive_markdown/models.py::SectionNode` \u2014 `SectionNode` parent/child tree model used by `MarkdownIndexer`; the stack algorithm at `indexer.py` lines 186\u2013247 is the reuse target. No change to `SectionNode` expected. | Risk: LOW | Why: Read-only reuse of existing model.\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Producers\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/ordinal_mapper.py::OrdinalPathMapper.build_map` \u2014 currently terminates at 2-level ordinals; must be extended to emit level-3+ sub-heading ordinals (e.g. `\\\"4.0.1\\\"`) and code-fence navigation tokens (e.g. `\\\"4.0.code.0\\\"`). `_extract_entry_title` / `_extract_preview` helpers unchanged; new recursive heading-scan loop added. | Risk: HIGH | Why: Core algorithm change \u2014 every MAP response is generated here; incorrect ordinal emission corrupts navigation for all consumers.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/disclosure_handler.py::BacklogViewDisclosureHandler.handle` \u2014 NAVIGATE path must resolve level-3 ordinals to sub-heading content slices and code-fence navigation tokens to fence bodies. ADR-5 module-reference invariant must be preserved. | Risk: HIGH | Why: Central dispatch \u2014 NAVIGATE mode for all sub-heading and code-fence requests routes through here; incorrect resolution returns wrong content or OrdinalNotFoundError.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/disclosure_types.py` \u2014 code-fence navigation tokens (`[code:N.M.code.K]`) may require a new response variant or extended `OrdinalEntry` fields (e.g. `entry_type: Literal[\\\"heading\\\",\\\"code_fence\\\",\\\"prose\\\"]`). If `MapResponse.map_text` format changes, all consumers of that field are affected. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Type contract shared with `server.py`, `test_disclosure_handler.py`, and `test_ordinal_mapper.py`; any new exported name must be added to `__all__`.\\n\\n### Codebase Audit\\n\\nplugin-context: YES (`plugins/` path component). All reuse must remain within `plugins/development-harness/` \u2014 cross-bundle imports break after installation.\\n\\n**Q1: Does `ordinal_mapper.py` currently parse entry content for sub-structure?**\\n\\n[CROSS-ANGLE] **NO.** `OrdinalPathMapper.build_map()` terminates at exactly 2 levels. The entry loop (lines 246\u2013268) calls only `_extract_entry_title(content)` (reads first non-empty line; strips `#` markers if heading) and `_extract_preview(content)` (reads first non-heading line). Entry content is a flat blob. The ordinal string `\\\"N.M.K\\\"` is accepted by the validation regex but never produced by `build_map()`.\\n\\nSource: `ordinal_mapper.py` lines 86\u2013130, 246\u2013268 (read 2026-06-01)\\n\\n**Q2: Does `progressive_markdown/navigator.py` or `list_navigator.py` contain reusable heading parsing?**\\n\\n[CROSS-ANGLE] **YES \u2014 the reusable infrastructure is in `progressive_markdown/indexer.py`**, not the navigator or list_navigator.\\n\\n- `progressive_markdown/navigator.py` (`ProgressiveMarkdownNavigator`): delegates parsing to `MarkdownItParser` and indexing to `MarkdownIndexer`. Does not parse headings directly.\\n- `progressive_markdown/list_navigator.py` (`ProgressiveDisclosure`, `chunk_text`): operates on list-of-dict data. No markdown heading parsing. Exports `ENCODING` and `TOKEN_BUDGET` constants used by `ordinal_mapper.py`.\\n- `progressive_markdown/indexer.py` (`MarkdownIndexer`): full recursive heading-tree builder. Accepts any markdown blob via `MarkdownItParser.parse()` + `MarkdownIndexer.build()`. Builds parent/child `SectionNode` tree, extracts code fences with IDs/language/content/summary. Stack algorithm at lines 186\u2013247 is directly applicable to depth-first ordinal assignment.\\n\\n**Key entry point for reuse:** `ProgressiveMarkdownNavigator.from_markdown(markdown: str)` (navigator.py lines 113\u2013131) \u2014 factory classmethod that takes a raw markdown string, returns a fully indexed, navigable document. Can be called on a single `NormalizedEntry.content` blob.\\n\\nSource: `progressive_markdown/indexer.py` lines 135\u2013324, `progressive_markdown/navigator.py` lines 113\u2013131 (read 2026-06-01)\\n\\n**Q3: Test fixtures that would need extension:**\\n\\n- `test_ordinal_mapper.py` `groomed_body_doc` fixture (lines 128\u2013169): section [4] entries already contain `\\\"\\n\\n### Concerns\\n\\n- [x] Pre-existing test failure: `test_init_docstring` (1 failure, 407/408 total) \u2014 RESOLVED: added docstring entries for MarkdownIndexer, MarkdownItParser, ParserResult to progressive_markdown/__init__.py; test now passes. (fixed in complete-implementation pass)\\n\\n### Configuration / CI\\n\\n- `pyproject.toml` (root) and `plugins/development-harness/pyproject.toml` \u2014 neither includes `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/` in testpaths. All new disclosure pipeline tests must be added to at least one testpaths list, or a new CI step must invoke `uv run pytest plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/` explicitly. | Risk: HIGH | Why: Without testpaths update, CI passes even when disclosure pipeline tests fail \u2014 the quality gate is blind to this subsystem.\\n- `.github/workflows/code-quality.yml` \u2014 runs `uv run -q pytest` against root pyproject.toml testpaths; does not currently cover `backlog_core/tests/`. | Risk: HIGH | Why: CI will not catch regressions in the disclosure pipeline tests; new behavior goes unverified in the pipeline.\\n\\n### Conflicts\\n\\nNone detected.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Cross-Angle Signals\\n\\n| Finding | Angles | Confidence |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `OrdinalPathMapper.build_map()` terminates at 2 levels; no entry sub-parsing | API State + Codebase Auditor | High |\\n| `progressive_markdown/indexer.py` (`MarkdownIndexer`) contains full reusable heading-parsing and code-fence-extraction infrastructure | Codebase Auditor + Impact Measurement | High |\\n| `NormalizedEntry.content: str` is always a flat raw markdown blob | API State + Codebase Auditor | High |\\n| Ordinal validation regex `^(\\\\d+\\\\.)*\\\\d+$` already accepts arbitrary depth | API State + Codebase Auditor | High |\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n\\n\\n### Documentation\\n\\n- `docs/mcp-progressive-disclosure-contract.md` \u2014 currently documents `4.0.1` as a possible ordinal (\\\"Second sub-heading within that entry \u2014 rare\\\") but the code never emits it today. When this feature ships, level-3 ordinals become real. Must add: (a) confirmed description of sub-heading ordinal format at all depths; (b) code-fence navigation token format (`[code:N.M.code.K]` or equivalent); (c) updated MAP response example showing level-3 rows. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Agent-facing contract document \u2014 inaccurate examples cause agent navigation errors.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md` \u2014 documents the disclosure pipeline phases (T14 `OrdinalPathMapper`, T16 `DisclosureRequestParser`, T17/T18). Adding recursive sub-heading logic and `MarkdownIndexer` reuse to `ordinal_mapper.py` adds a new pipeline step. Architecture section will become stale. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Used by implementers and reviewers to understand pipeline structure; stale descriptions cause incorrect changes.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/.claude/agent-memory/python-engineering-python-cli-architect/project_progressive_markdown.md` \u2014 agent memory recording `progressive_markdown` architecture state; will be outdated once `MarkdownIndexer` is integrated into the backlog_core disclosure pipeline. | Risk: LOW | Why: Agent memory, not runtime behavior; affects future agent planning, not current execution.\\n- `.claude/agent-memory/python-engineering-adversarial-solution-design/backlog_view_architecture.md` \u2014 records backlog_view architecture; will become stale on level-3 ordinal emission. | Risk: LOW | Why: Agent memory only.\\n\\n### Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\n\\n- [ ] Every code producer updated or verified compatible (`ordinal_mapper.py`, `disclosure_handler.py`, `disclosure_types.py`)\\n- [ ] Every code consumer migrated or verified compatible (`server.py`, `content_normalizer.py`, `list_navigator.py`)\\n- [ ] Every affected test updated or new coverage added (`test_ordinal_mapper.py`, `test_disclosure_handler.py`, integration test)\\n- [ ] Every stale document updated (`docs/mcp-progressive-disclosure-contract.md`, `ARCHITECTURE.md`)\\n- [ ] Every affected config or CI path updated and validated (`pyproject.toml` testpaths, `code-quality.yml`)\\n- [ ] Every affected agent or skill instruction updated (`backlog-mcp-validator.md`)\\n- [ ] Replacement or migration coverage gaps resolved (code-fence token format / ordinal regex compatibility confirmed)\\n- [ ] Hidden transitive dependencies checked (`MarkdownIndexer` cross-subpackage import within plugin bundle verified safe)\\n\\n### Ecosystem and Compatibility\\n\\n**Internal module \u2014 no external community.** External ecosystem findings limited to library availability.\\n\\n**Markdown parsing libraries already in the codebase:**\\n- `markdown-it-py` \u2014 already used in `progressive_markdown/parser.py` (`MarkdownItParser`). Full token stream (`heading_open`, `fence` tokens) already available through existing `MarkdownItParser.parse()` \u2192 `MarkdownIndexer.build()` pipeline.\\n- `marko` \u2014 specified in project CLAUDE.md for markdown AST parsing tasks; available as dev dependency. Lighter alternative if a full `markdown-it-py` parse is not desired.\\n\\n**Critical gotcha \u2014 heading exclusion in code blocks:**\\n`MarkdownIndexer` uses `markdown-it-py`'s `heading_open` tokens. Headings inside fenced code blocks are excluded by construction \u2014 they appear as `fence` content tokens, not `heading_open` tokens. Sub-heading parsing of entry content via `MarkdownIndexer` naturally excludes headings-inside-code. Source: `progressive_markdown/indexer.py` lines 135\u2013140, read 2026-06-01.\\n\\n**Critical gotcha \u2014 coordinate system mismatch:**\\n`ProgressiveMarkdownNavigator` uses its own coordinate system (`sec_0001` IDs, `h2.1.2` selectors, `code_0001` code block IDs). These differ from the disclosure pipeline's dot-path ordinal system (`N.M.K`). Using `MarkdownIndexer` to parse entry sub-structure requires mapping `SectionNode` tree positions to the dot-path ordinal namespace \u2014 this bridging is not automatic. Source: `progressive_markdown/models.py` (SectionNode), `progressive_markdown/navigator.py` lines 304\u2013350, read 2026-06-01.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Gaps (cross-angle)\\n\\nBoth impact-measurement and codebase-auditor identified the same unverified surface: **whether existing JSON fixture files (`issue-2515-full.json`, etc.) contain entries with `##`/`###` sub-headings or code fences in practice.** JSON files were not directly inspected. This determines whether new synthetic test fixtures are needed or existing fixtures already provide level-3 coverage material.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Gaps (single-angle)\\n\\n- **Impact Measurement**: No benchmark data for `markdown-it-py` parse overhead per entry. Relevant if a level-3 emission gate requires a cost-based threshold.\\n- **Codebase Auditor**: `backlog_core/tests/integration/test_backlog_view_disclosure_tool.py` not fully audited \u2014 full disclosure integration test scope unknown.\\n- **Ecosystem**: Whether `marko` or `markdown-it-py` is preferred for entry-content sub-parsing in this context is not documented. CLAUDE.md specifies `marko` generally; `markdown-it-py` is already integrated in `progressive_markdown/`. No explicit guidance for this specific use case.\\n\\n### Issue Classification\\n\\n**Type**: unbounded-design\\n\\n**Rationale**: The problem space is not yet bounded. The original `backlog_view` disclosure design shipped with a 2-level navigation contract, but structured reports (RT-ICA, groomed sections, design decisions) naturally use `###` sub-headings as granularity units. When agents navigate to these sections, they receive the entire blob, defeating progressive disclosure. Multiple valid approaches exist to resolve this: (1) implement recursive navigation at arbitrary depth, (2) validate that disclosure sections are leaf-level only, (3) restructure report storage, or (4) require agents to specify sub-section names. The item requires design framing to evaluate these options and select the best approach.\\n\\n**Analysis Method**: design-framing\\n\\n**Scenario Target**: Agent receives large structured report sections with sub-headings as unnavigable blobs, defeating progressive disclosure \u2192 Agent can navigate recursively to any depth, see sub-headings in disclosure menu, and request specific sub-sections without receiving unwanted context overhead\\n\\n### Quantitative Impact\\n\\n**Baseline file sizes** (measured via `wc -c`, 2026-06-01):\\n\\n| Artifact | Chars | Est. tokens (\u00f73.5) |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `ordinal_mapper.py` | 12,868 | ~3,677 |\\n| `disclosure_handler.py` | 21,758 | ~6,217 |\\n| `content_normalizer.py` | 8,839 | ~2,525 |\\n| `progressive_markdown/indexer.py` | 11,992 | ~3,426 |\\n| `progressive_markdown/navigator.py` | 20,031 | ~5,723 |\\n| `progressive_markdown/list_navigator.py` | 23,986 | ~6,853 |\\n| `progressive_markdown/models.py` | 12,726 | ~3,636 |\\n| `progressive_markdown/codeblocks.py` | 4,523 | ~1,292 |\\n| `progressive_markdown/` (total all files) | 158,678 | ~45,337 |\\n\\n**Test infrastructure:**\\n- `test_ordinal_mapper.py`: ~799 lines, 6 test classes (TC-O1\u2013TC-O6), uses `issue-2515-full.json`.\\n- `test_disclosure_handler.py`: 5 test classes (TC-H1\u2013TC-H5), TDD against `BacklogViewDisclosureHandler`.\\n- JSON fixtures available: `issue-2515-full.json` (53 sections), `issue-1857-full.json`, `issue-2521-full.json`, `issue-996-full.json`.\\n\\n**Delta estimate for 2-level \u2192 arbitrary depth extension** [pre-implementation]:\\n- `ordinal_mapper.py` additions: ~100\u2013200 lines (~600\u20131,200 chars / ~170\u2013340 tokens est.)\\n- New test methods for level-3 ordinals: ~50\u2013100 lines per test class (~1,500\u20133,000 chars / ~430\u2013860 tokens est.)\\n\\n**Map growth model:** Current map O(N + N\u00d7M). With arbitrary depth, worst case O(N + N\u00d7M + N\u00d7M\u00d7K) where K = sub-headings per entry. A level-3 emission gate analogous to the level-2 gate is needed to keep map output under the architect spec \u00a75.6 2,000-token ceiling for 53-section items.\\n\\n**Performance impact:** No benchmarks found in `ordinal_mapper.py`, `disclosure_handler.py`, `progressive_markdown/`, or `tests/`. No `perf/` or `benchmark/` directories exist. Parsing entry content via `MarkdownIndexer` adds one `markdown-it-py` parse per entry that triggers level-3 emission.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Research \u2014 backlog_core disclosure pipeline / recursive sub-heading navigation and code-fence extraction \u2014 2026-06-01\\n\\nResearch method: 4-angle independent fan-out (api-state, ecosystem-research, impact-measurement, codebase-auditor). [CROSS-ANGLE] marks findings confirmed by 2+ independent angles. No conflicts detected.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Risk Summary\\n\\n- Overall system risk: **MEDIUM**\\n- Highest-risk systems:\\n  - `backlog_core/ordinal_mapper.py` \u2014 core algorithm change; incorrect ordinal emission corrupts MAP responses for all callers\\n  - `backlog_core/tests/unit/test_ordinal_mapper.py` + `test_disclosure_handler.py` \u2014 no level-3 test coverage exists and the test directory is outside CI testpaths\\n  - `pyproject.toml` / `.github/workflows/code-quality.yml` \u2014 `backlog_core/tests/` is not in CI; new behavior is unverified by the quality gate\\n- Main risk themes:\\n  - **verification**: no level-3 ordinal tests exist; CI is blind to `backlog_core/tests/`\\n  - **behavioral**: code-fence navigation token format is novel \u2014 request parser regex `^(\\\\d+\\\\.)*\\\\d+$` only matches digits and dots, which code-fence tokens (`\\\"4.0.code.0\\\"`) may violate\\n  - **scope**: `MarkdownIndexer` reuse introduces a new import dependency from `backlog_core` into `progressive_markdown` (cross-subpackage); must be verified as safe within the plugin bundle\\n- Scope expansion:\\n  - **CI testpath gap** discovered: `backlog_core/tests/` (containing all disclosure pipeline tests) is absent from both `pyproject.toml` testpaths. This is a pre-existing infrastructure gap that this feature makes critical \u2014 new behavior lands without CI coverage. Requires testpaths update as part of this item's scope.\\n  - 